 dict(pmid='43035818', doi='10.1002/ajhb.70356', journal='Am J Hum Biol', title='Poorer Air Quality During Trimesters 2 and 3 of Gestation Is Associated With Decreased Bone Mineral Density in Childhood and Adolescence.', short='New Mexico 597 paediatric decedents postmortem CT bone mineral density gestational AQI trimester', design='Cross-sectional', pm='County-level Air Quality Index over the 9 months before birth, by trimester; ambient temperature extremes', geo='United States (New Mexico)', endpoint='Bone mineral density and porous cranial lesions on postmortem CT', tier='C', basis='Reference / regulatory monitor', affil='University of New Mexico Health Sciences Center, USA; University of New Mexico, USA', n='Postmortem CT scans of 597 paediatric decedents (6 months to 20.9 years) in New Mexico, 2011-2023, linked to county-level AQI and temperature during the 9 months before birth. Lower BMD was associated with poorer AQI in trimesters 2 and 3, trimester 2 being the candidate sensitive window; AQI was unrelated to porous cranial lesions and temperature extremes to either outcome. No effect sizes in the abstract. AQI is a composite dominated by whichever pollutant is worst on a day, assigned at county level, and a decedent sample is not representative of living children. Relevance: indirect - county AQI is the coarsest exposure surrogate; a pollutant-specific, address-level PM2.5 estimate is the obvious next step.', sub=corpus.REPR),
